    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the number of different starting lineups for Coach Grunt's basketball team, we need to consider the constraints and the reasoning step by step.\n\nGiven:\n- There are 12 players on the team.\n- Two of them, Ace and Zeppo, will definitely be in the starting lineup.\n\nWe need to find the number of different ways to choose the remaining 3 players from the remaining 10 players (since Ace and Zeppo are already chosen).\n\nThis is a combination problem where the order does not matter. The number of ways to choose \\( k \\) items from \\( n \\) items is given by the combination formula:\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n\\]\nHere, \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\).\n\nLet's calculate the number of combinations:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!} = \\frac{10!}{3! \\cdot 7!}\n\\]\n\nFirst, simplify the factorials:\n\\[\n10! = 10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7!\n\\]\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\\[\n7! = 7!\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7!}{3! \\times 7!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{6}\n\\]\n\nNow, perform the multiplication and division:\n\\[\n\\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{6} = \\frac{720}{6} = 120\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different starting lineups possible is \\(\\boxed{120}\\).",
