    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of different starting lineups possible for the basketball team, we need to select 3 more players from the remaining 10 players (since Ace and Zeppo are already chosen) and then arrange them in a lineup. Here is the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Select 3 players from the remaining 10 players:**\n   We need to choose 3 players out of the 10 remaining players. The number of ways to choose 3 players from 10 is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\), where \\( n \\) is the total number of items to choose from, and \\( k \\) is the number of items to choose. Here, \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!} = \\frac{10!}{3! \\cdot 7!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7!}{3! \\times 7!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 120\n   \\]\n\n2. **Arrange the 3 chosen players in a lineup:**\n   Once we have chosen the 3 players, we need to arrange them in a specific order to form a lineup. The number of ways to arrange 3 players is given by the permutation formula \\( P(n, k) = \\frac{n!}{(n-k)!} \\), where \\( n \\) is the total number of items to arrange. Here, \\( n = 3 \\).\n\n   \\[\n   P(3, 3) = \\frac{3!}{(3-3)!} = \\frac{3!}{0!} = \\frac{3!}{1} = 3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the total number of different starting lineups:**\n   The total number of different starting lineups is the product of the number of ways to choose the players and the number of ways to arrange them.\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of different starting lineups} = \\binom{10}{3} \\times P(3, 3) = 120 \\times 6 = 720\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different starting lineups possible is \\(\\boxed{720}\\).",
